course: dictionary
lesson: 2008
title: Strong words that judge
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To condemn something is to say very clearly and firmly that it is wrong or bad. «Leaders condemn the attack» means the leaders say the attack was wrong, and they say it in public so everyone hears.
explain: Condemn can also be a court word. «In 399 BCE a jury in Athens condemned Socrates to death» means the court gave him a punishment. The clue is «to death», which tells you what kind of punishment it was.
explain: Condemn can also mean forced into a bad situation, or a building that is officially not safe. Check your meaning by swapping in «judged as wrong» and seeing if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Condemn means to say something is wrong or bad, strongly and in public.
fact: «Condemned» can mean sentenced by a court, as in «condemned to death».
fact: A «condemned building» is officially not safe to use, so it must be closed or knocked down.
fact: «In 399 BCE a jury in Athens condemned him for "corrupting the young", and he chose not to escape from prison.»
fact: In ethics, you can condemn an act but not hate the person who did it.
exercise: «Leaders strongly condemn the attack.» What does condemn mean here? | To celebrate it with a party | To forget it quickly | To film it for the news | *To say it is wrong and bad
exercise: «In 399 BCE a jury in Athens condemned Socrates to death.» What does condemned mean here? | Set free from prison | Given a prize | *Found guilty and given a punishment by a court | Asked to write a poem
exercise: What is a «condemned building»? | A building with lots of windows | A brand new school | A building painted in bright colours | *A building officially not safe to use
test: In Christian teaching, to condemn someone means to judge them guilty of sin. The Bible says Jesus came to do what? | To write books | To build churches | *To save people | To condemn people
test: «Condemn the act but not hate the person.» What does this suggest? | Praise the act and the person | *Say an act is wrong without hating the person who did it | Hate the person and the act | Ignore the act completely
exam: «Acquitting the guilty and condemning the innocent: the Lord detests them both» (Proverbs 17:15). Which word means the opposite of condemning? | Sentencing | *Acquitting | Hiding | Warning
